<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Thuringians</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 531 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Germanic kingdom in central Germany, briefly one of the great powers of the post-Roman west until the Franks destroyed it in 531.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Thuringians appear around 400 in a Roman veterinary text that praises their horses. They likely descended in part from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hermunduri">Hermunduri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, and formed a kingdom centred on the Thuringian basin, extending at its height from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the early 6th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herminafrid">Herminafrid</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalaberga">Amalaberga</a>, niece of the Ostrogothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> to the Ostrogothic alliance system. Rich graves, horse burials and the trade in horses show an aristocratic warrior society. Little is known of its institutions, as it left no written records of its own.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric</a>'s death removed Ostrogothic protection, the Frankish kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-i">Theuderic I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> defeated the Thuringians on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> in 531; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herminafrid">Herminafrid</a> was killed soon after at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a>. The north went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> allies, the rest to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a>, later forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 400 CE – 531 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.66%;width:2.61%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 531 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bisinus, Herminafrid, Berthachar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thuringian (Elbe Germanic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thuringii, Thuringians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=466&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-thuringians" aria-label="Kingdom of the Thuringians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 428 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Thuringians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5228 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 684h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5225 684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=466&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-thuringians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
